## What happened

Starting in **.NET 9**, `DSA.Create()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on **macOS, iOS, and tvOS**. Apple's Security.framework never supported DSA; earlier .NET versions used a built-in OpenSSL shim, but Microsoft removed that fallback. Your .NET 10 build likely pinned an older runtime or SDK that still carried it — .NET 11 does not.

## How to fix it

Since you only **verify** existing signatures (not generate new ones), you have two practical options:

### Option 1 — Use BouncyCastle (recommended)

```bash
dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
```

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

bool VerifyDsa(byte[] message, byte[] signature, DsaPublicKeyParameters publicKey)
{
    var signer = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA-1withDSA");
    signer.Init(forSigning: false, publicKey);
    signer.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
    return signer.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

This is pure managed code — works on every platform with no native dependencies.

### Option 2 — Guard with a runtime check

If you can limit DSA verification to Windows/Linux only:

```csharp
if (!OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
{
    using var dsa = DSA.Create();
    // ...
}
else
{
    throw new PlatformNotSupportedException(
        "DSA verification requires BouncyCastle on macOS.");
}
```

### Long-term

DSA is considered legacy everywhere. If you control the signature format, migrate to **ECDSA** or **Ed25519** (`ECDsa.Create()` / .NET 11's `EdDSA` support), which are supported on all platforms.
